# Seasonal variability
### Is precipitation seasonally varying?

We aim to investigate the variability of Scottish perception and temperature data across a span of approximately sixty years by first loading the data $\textbf{ghcnd_stations}$ and $\textbf{ghcnd_values}$, and then combine them with the `left_jiont` function into a new data frame called `ghcnd`. To facilitate analysis, a single year was selected for examination, specifically the year 2008. We filter the data on `Year == 2008` and create two plots to displaying the daily precipitation and temperature behaviors respectively. 

Generally, we first focus on the analysis on precipitation data by filtering on `Element == "PRCP"`.
The result is shown on scatter plot where each dot represent the precipitation data for the specific day and there is a smooth line in darkblue shows the monthly average precipitation across the whole year. To be more specific, the plot shows the result for each station by applying `facet_wrap` function.



```{r, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE}
# load the data from the package
data(ghcnd_stations, package = "StatCompLab")
data(ghcnd_values, package = "StatCompLab")

# Creat a data set that joint two origrinal datafram together
ghcnd <- left_join(ghcnd_values, ghcnd_stations, by = "ID")

# Filter data for the selected year(2008)
ghcnd_2008 <- filter(ghcnd, Year == 2008)

# Filter the data for 2008 perception
ghcnd_2008_prcp <- ghcnd_2008 %>%
  filter(Element == "PRCP")

# Create plot
ggplot(ghcnd_2008_prcp, aes(x = DecYear, y = Value, color = Element), size = 1.2) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(method = "loess", color = "darkblue", size = 0.7) +
  labs(x = "Decimal Year",
       y = "Precipitation (mm)",
       title = paste0("Daily precipitation for ", 2008)) +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(size = 7)) + 
  facet_wrap(~Name)

```


Here we set April to September as summer and other month to be winter. The trend on these plots indicate that several station have more volatility, for instance Ardtalnaig and Benmore: Younger Bontanic Garden, whereas the others are more smooth. To be more specifically, Ardtalnaig and Benmore: Younger Bontanic Garden station exhibit higher amounts of precipitation during winter months and lower amounts during summer months, whereas the opposite is true for the other stations, except for Braemar, where the precipitation is consistent throughout the year.


```{r, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE}
# Filter the data for 2008 temperature
ghcnd_2008_tem <- ghcnd_2008 %>%
  filter(Element %in% c("TMAX", "TMIN"))

# Create plot
ggplot(ghcnd_2008_tem, aes(x = DecYear, y = Value, color = Element)) +
  geom_point(size = 0.8) +
  labs(x = "Decimal Year",
       y = "Temperature (°C)",
       title = paste0("Daily minimum and maximum temperature for ", 2008)) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c(TMAX = "dodgerblue3", TMIN = "darkorange1")) +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(size = 6.5)) + 
  facet_wrap(~Name)

```


Similarly to the previous step for precipitation, we select the temperature data by filtering on `Element %in% c("TMAX", "TMIN")`. It is evident that there is a seasonal effect with the temperature being always higher in summer and lower in winter. this trend was observed in all stations which  exactly coincide with the seasonal effect above. 

To further analyze the precipitation, there is Monte Carlo permutation test for the hypotheses that we want to test:
$$
\begin{align*}
& H_0: \text{The rainfall distribution is the same in winter as in summer} \\
& H_1: \text{The winter and summer distributions have different expected values}
\end{align*}
$$
We define the test statistics as the absoluate value of the difference between average precipitation in winter and summer for each station respectively as $T = |\text{winter average} - \text{summer average}|$. For convinence, we add a  $\textbf{Summer}$ column to the data that is $\textbf{TRUE}$ for summer month and $\textbf{FALSE}$ for winter month by the `mutate` function. We first calculate the test statistics $T$ for each station. 

```{r, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE}
ghcnd_selected <- ghcnd %>%
  filter(Element == "PRCP") %>%
  group_by(Name, ID, Year, Day, Month, Element) %>%
  summarise(Value = mean(Value), .groups = "drop") %>%
  mutate("Summer" = Month %in% c(4:9))

# Compute winter and summer averages for each station
# Winter
winter_avg <- ghcnd_selected %>%
  filter(Summer == FALSE) %>%
  group_by(Name, ID) %>%
  summarise(winter_average = mean(Value), .groups = "drop")

# Summer
summer_avg <- ghcnd_selected %>%
  filter(Summer == TRUE) %>%
  group_by(Name, ID) %>%
  summarise(summer_average = mean(Value), .groups = "drop")

# Calculate the test statistice
T_stats <- abs(winter_avg$winter_average - summer_avg$summer_average)
# Summaries the data in the table
test_table <- winter_avg %>%
  mutate(summer_average = summer_avg$summer_average, T = T_stats)

kable(test_table, align = "lcccc")
```


We do a randomized permutation test, since we have no model for the distribution of the data, by defining a function `p_value_CI` which calculate the p-value, Monte Carlo standard deviation and the confidence interval for p-value. Then, we count the number of times when the observed value($T_{obs}$) is greater than or equal to the test statistic($T$), $T_{obs} \ge T$. Further, the Porject2Hint document provided the model for the count, $X$, and the p-value as
$$
X \sim \text{Bin}(N, p),  \quad \text{and}\quad p = \mathbb{P}(T_{obs} \ge T | H_0).
$$
where $N$ represents the number of permutations we take. Then we have 
$\mathbb{E}(X) = Np$, and $\text{Var}(X) = Np(1-p)$. To do the further evaluation about the value of $p$, we can estimate it with $\hat{p} = x/N$ and thus implied that the expected value and variance of $\hat{p}$ are
$$
\mathbb{E}(\hat{p}) = p, \quad \text{and}\quad \text{Var}(\hat{p}) = \frac{p(1-p)}{N}.
$$
Therefore, computing the p-values for each station and determining their Monte Carlo standard deviation can be accomplished by calculating $x/N$ and taking the square root of their variance, which is given by $\sqrt{\frac{p(1-p)}{N}}$. Moreover, with sufficiently large values of $N$, we can calculate the $95\%$ confidence interval for the p-values by
$$
\text{CI} = \hat{p}\pm z_{0.975}\sqrt{\frac{p(1-p)}{N}}
$$
Despite that most of the observed values of count is zero, resulting in a p-value approaching zero due to a fixed $N>0$, we can instead calculate a confidence interval for this case by the interval $[0, 1-0.0025^{1/N}]$. By applying the `p_value_CI` function, the resulting table shows the p-value, Monte Carlo standard deviation, and corresponding confidence interval for the p-value of each station.

```{r, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE}
p_value_CI(ghcnd_selected)
```


Here we set $N=10000$ and rejected the null hypothesis for each station where the corresponding p-value is less than 0.05. The table shows that most stations have a p-value of zero, but EDINBURGH: ROYAL BOTANIC GARDE and LEUCHARS have positive p-values. Notably, the p-value for EDINBURGH: ROYAL BOTANIC GARDE is surprisingly large, which indicates that we failed to reject the null hypothesis of no seasonal difference in rainfall distribution. Conversely, for LEUCHARS, the p-value is positive but less than 0.05, allowing us to reject the null hypothesis. Therefore, except for EDINBURGH: ROYAL BOTANIC GARDE, we reject the null hypothesis for all stations, indicating that a seasonal difference in rainfall distribution exists at these seven stations.




# Spatial weather prediction
### Estimation and prediction

For the second part of the project, we begin by modifying our data frame with the code below. We calculate the square root of the monthly averaged precipitation value and store it as a new variable called $\textbf{Value_sqrt_avg}$. This generates a new data frame $\textbf{ghcnd_spatial}$.
```{r, eval=TRUE, echo=TRUE}
# Construct a version of the data set with a new variable, Value_sqrt_avg
# Subset the data to include only precipitation values
ghcnd_prcp<- ghcnd %>%
  filter(Element == "PRCP")
# Group by name, year, and month and compute the average value
ghcnd_spatial <- ghcnd_prcp %>%
  group_by(ID, Year, Month) %>%
  summarise(DecYear = mean(DecYear), Value_monthly = mean(Value), .groups = "drop") %>%
  mutate(Value_sqrt_avg = sqrt(Value_monthly))
```


Now there is a basic model for the square root of monthly average precipitation defined as 
$$
M_0: \text{Value_sqrt_avg} \sim  \text{Intercept + Longtitude + Latitude + Elevation + DecYear}
$$
Then by adding covariates $\cos{(2\pi kt)}$ and $\sin{(2\pi kt)}$ of frequencey $k=1,2,3$... we can also model the seasonal variability, defining four models $M_1$, $M_2$, $M_3$, and $M_4$, where the predictor expression for model $M_K$ adds 
$$
\sum_{k=1}^{K} [\gamma_{c,k} \cos{(2\pi kt)} + \gamma_{s,k} \sin{(2\pi kt)}]
$$
to the $M_0$ predictor expression, for the model coefficient $\gamma_{c,k}$ and $\gamma_{s,k}$. Moreover, the time $t$ here is defined to be the $\textbf{DecYear}$. 

Now we aim to estimate the model parameters for all five models. We defined the function `fit_model` which takes the value of the corresponding data frame and the value of k, which respond to each model, and return the result of the fitted model. Further, with the help of `summary` function, we finally obtain the estimation of parameters for all models.

```{r, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE}
ghcnd_data <- left_join(ghcnd_spatial, ghcnd_stations, by = "ID")

# Fit the model for M0, M1, M2, M3, and M4
m0 <- kable(summary(fit_model(data = ghcnd_data, k = 0))$coefficient)
m1 <- kable(summary(fit_model(data = ghcnd_data, k = 1))$coefficient)
m2 <- kable(summary(fit_model(data = ghcnd_data, k = 2))$coefficient)
m3 <- kable(summary(fit_model(data = ghcnd_data, k = 3))$coefficient)
m4 <- kable(summary(fit_model(data = ghcnd_data, k = 4))$coefficient)
```


For model $M_0$ below, we found the estimate for longitude and latitude are both negative, which suggests that there is a slight decrease in precipitation as we move westwards and northwards. Moreover, the estimate for elevation and DecYear are both positive and very small, indicating that there is a slight increase in precipitation over the year with increasing elevation.


